You do not need to know how to program in Xsl or other languages.
You will create an awk database that will tell the program what needs to be programmed.
An Awk database is written in plain text and is human readable and writabale.
Each line is a record.
Each line contains fields that are ordinarily delineated by spaces.
Below is an example:

 Fields:FirstName LastName City State
Record1:James Marsh Chicago IL
Record2:William Jones Phoenix AZ	    
Record3:Robert DeVita Denver CO
Record4:Thomas Morse New York NY

As you can see Record4 presents a problem:There is a space in New York and therefore Record4 has 5 fields instead of 4.

Awk provides a way to change the delimiter of the fields to say ":".
Thus Record4 could be written Thomas:Morse:New York:NY

I have chosen whitespace! as my delimiter.
The above database would be written by me (or you or the program) as:
James	  !Marsh   !Chicago !IL
William	  !Jones   !Phoenix !AZ
Robert	  !DeVita  !Denver  !CO
Thomas	  !Morse   !New York!NY



The layout section is concerned with two things.
The main one is placing the data in the right place on the page.
The second is to handle the text data for the form.

The text data is either a tlin or a tPull line.
The tlin is one into which the taxpayor enters data.
The tPull is one which pulls its data from another form.
You will need to make certain that this pull is performed properly.

The layout section has as its final product the file ../2Fo/FormName_Copy00.2fo.

Your menu is in your Main directory where you are now.
The menu is called layoutmenu.
It is invoked from the Main directory with the ./layoutmenu command.
Note the leading ./ because it is assumed that the working directory
is not in your PATH for security reasons.
You will have to enlarge the window to accomodate the menu.

The background is the jpg image of the form produced in ../../1pdf2jpg.
You will copy this file to your ../Jpg folder. (Menu choice 1a)

The nomenclature of the lines is determined in ../../2sequence.
You will copy the file ../../2sequence/Text/FormName.txt to ../Text.
(This is menu choice 1b.)

MENU CHOICE 1c allows you to edit this file.
You may have to remove any extraneous !'s.
You should remove the heading line.

MENU CHOICE 1d moves the columns around.
The .txt file had four columns: type, name, seq and blank.
The columns will be rearranged to: blank, type, name and seq.
The resulting file will be ../Columns/FormName.col.

MENU CHOICE 1e allows you to edit ../Columns/FormName.col.
Visualize your layout as consisting of columns and lines.

The columns all run from the top of the page to the bottom.
They have two measurement: left and right.
These indicate the left and right edges of the column.

The lines are placed in a column.
They have only one measurement: top.
This indicates how far down the page in the column the line is.

The first column in your .col file will contain a letter a-z.
This means you can have 26 columns on your page.

Put an a in the first column of the first line.
Then above it enter a line for the column.
It will be:a  !col
Your file will look like this:
a    !col
a    !tPull	!TFNI	!001
     !tPull	!SFNI	!002
     !tlin	!addr1	!003
     !tlin	!addr2	!004
     !dlin	!01	!005

Now you decide that SFNI belongs right below TFNI.
That is, it is in the same column.
You simply place an a in the first column of SFNI.

You decide that the addr lines belong in a different column.
You place a b in front of addr1 and addr2.
You designate a column b by making a line for it.
Your file will now look like this:

a    !col
b    !col
a    !tPull	!TFNI	!001
a    !tPull	!SFNI	!002
b    !tlin	!addr1	!003
b    !tlin	!addr2	!004
     !dlin	!01	!005

You continue in this manner,
adding columns at the top and
placing lower case letters in front of each line.

MENU CHOICE 1f will sort the file based on the first column.
The result will be ../Sorted/FormName.sorted
The result will be an awk database:

a   !col
a   !tPull	!TFNI	!001
a   !tPull	!SFNI	!002
b   !col
b   !tlin	!addr1	!003
b   !tlin	!addr2	!004
c   !col
c   !dlin	!01	!005
etc.

MENU CHOICE 1g allows you to check this file for mistakes.

MENU CHOICE 1h, using awk, creates the outline of your page.
This file is ../PreLot/FormName.pre.
Simplified, it will look something like this:

Form
  pgS

    col
      col
        tlin
        tPull
        dlin
      Endcol****

      col
        tlin
        tPull
        dlin
      Endcol****

      col
        tlin
        tPull
        dlin
      Endcol****
    Endcol****
EndpgS
EndForm

Note that there are only three types of lines for you to deal with:
tlin are entries in the form where the taxpayor enters text.
tPull are lines where the text is drawn from other forms.
dlin are used here for all numerical lines regardless of their type.
They will be sorted into data entry, addition, subtraction, etc.
in the ../../4calc directory later.

The lines occupy one line.
All other entries are pairs that enclose something.

The outside enclosure is the Form EndForm pair.

The next inside enclosure is the pgS EndpgS pair enclosing a page.
The pgS EndpgS is used for single page form.
The pgF EndpgF is used for the first page of a 2 or more page form.
The pgM EndpgM is used for middle pages.
The pgL EndpgL is used for the last page of a 2 or more page form.

The next inside enclosure is a column that encloses all the othe columns.
This enclosure allows ALL the lines in the form to be moved up or down.
It is needed since the gimp data is about 0.10 inches too low.

Then comes the col Endcol***** pairs that enclose lines.

The page, column and line entries have an accompanying line above them.
This line tell you what data belongs where.
For instance, a col pair will look like this:

xcol   !2name !3top !4left    !5right	      
col    !      !	    !	      !


Endcol****

This guiding line is preceded by and x so that awk ignores it.

MENU CHOICE 1i allows you to check this file.

Note carefully that each pair has a start and an end.

Note carefully that each column encloses at least one line.
If a column does not enclose a line, an error is thrown.
This error comes with little information to guide you.
In fact, if you get an error with little or no information
to tell you what is wrong, most likely you have a column
that does not enclose a line.

Lines need a name.
Columns do not seem to need a name.
The top measurement of a column is always 0.00 inches.

If your form has more than one page, I would do each page separately
and then concatenate them together.

MENU CHOICE 1j copies the .pre file to ../Lot/FormName_Copy00.lot.reg
There are warnings and backups to try to prevent your overwriting
data that you had previously entered.

MENU CHOICE 1k opens gimp.
You should open your the ../Jpg/  image of the page on which you are working.
Change the measurements to inches rather than pixels.
Change the zoom to 50% or whatever is comfortable for you.

Place the pointer on the left side of the entry box and
record the x dimension value. This is the "left" side of your column.

Place the pointer on the right side of the entry box and
record the x dimension value. This is the "right" side of your column.

Place the pointer slightly above the bottom line of the entry box.
Record the y dimension value. This is the "top" measure for your line. 

MENU CHOICE 1l allows you to edit ../Lot/FormName_Copy00.lot.reg.
You have already looked it over in the .pre stage.
You can now enter the data that you have garnered from the gimp program.
You can run the gimp program simultaneously, but it is probably
easier to write the gimp data on paper and then enter it here.

The other data to be entered is:
1: A dummy value to be shown on the form.
2: For the tPull lines, enter the Form, Copy number (00), and line name
from which the data is to be pulled.

MENU CHOICE 1m makes a simplified version of your regular file.
../Lot/FormName_Copy00.lot.reg is converted to ../Lot/FormName_Copy00.lot.sim
That is all the tPull lines are changed to tlin lines.
This allows the use of dummy values on each line.
There is no dependence on other files.

MENU CHOICE 1n allows you to look at the .sim file to check it.

MENU CHOICE 1o copies the .sim file to ../Lot/FormName_Copy00.lot
This file is used for steps 2 through 9.
Menu choice 10 carries the file through all the 2-9 steps.
If it doesn't work, then you can step through each step individually
to see where things go wrong.

When your form looks the way you think it should,
you should see what happens when you use the .reg file instead of the .sim one.

MENU CHOICE 11 copies the .reg file to the .lot file.
MENU CHOICE 12 does what 10 did with the .sim file.

The regular .reg file contains tPull lines.
These lines will not show up unless the files from which
they draw their data exist.
These files are contained in ../Data directory.
Let's say one of your lines is the taxpayors last name.
This data is to be pulled from your background form Bkg.
Your form looks for this data in the file:
../Data/Bkg_Copy00_lineTLN_text.xml
If this file does not already exist you need to create it
in order to test whether your program will fetch the data.
Go to the ../Data directory. Copy any existing *_text.xml file
to Bkg_Copy00_lineTLN_text.xml.
Then edit that file so that the
<text></text> markup encloses the text you wish to be shown.
ie: <text>LastName</text>
Once this is done, menu choice 12 should show the pulled data.

MENU CHOICES 11 and 12 should ALWAYS be run at the end of your work.
This creates the ../2Fo/FormName_Copy00.2fo file that the rest of
the program depends upon. It should have the tPull lines in it.